# Nulang Grammar (EBNF)

> **Authoritative grammar for the Frozen (Core) and Stable tiers** of the
> Nulang language, per RFC 0002 (Frozen Core) and `GOVERNANCE.md` §2. This
> file is a Frozen-tier artifact: the Core productions below may not change
> without a new RFC and a major-version bump. The Stable productions may
> change only under the deprecation contract.
>
> The hand-rolled recursive-descent parser in `src/parser.rs` is the reference
> implementation of this grammar. The conformance corpus in
> `conformance/grammar/` (positive + negative examples) validates the parser
> against this grammar; see `conformance/README.md` for the check harness. The
> corpus deliberately exercises **parser reality**; where the parser and this
> grammar historically diverged, the drift is logged in
> `conformance/grammar/README.md` and dispositioned (spec-fix, parser-fix, or
> accepted leniency) in `docs/SPEC_RECONCILIATION.md`. The productions below
> reflect the round-1 and round-2 reconciliations.
>
> Experimental surface (actors' `@backend`, `workflow`, `agent`, FFI `extern`,
> Python interop, WIT) is **out of scope** for this grammar and is not part of
> the frozen or stable language.
>
> EBNF conventions: `{ X }` = zero or more; `[ X ]` = optional; `X | Y` =
> alternation; `"tok"` = literal terminal; `(* comment *)`. Newlines and
> semicolons are equivalent statement separators (`SEP`).

## 1. Lexical conventions

```
IDENT        = lowercase ( lowercase | uppercase | digit | "_" )*
UPPER_IDENT  = uppercase ( lowercase | uppercase | digit | "_" )*
INT_LIT      = digit { digit | "_" }
              | "0x" hex_digit { hex_digit }
              | "0o" octal_digit { octal_digit }
              | "0b" bit { bit }
                 (* the current lexer is more lenient than this: it accepts
                    out-of-range digits (`0o8`, `0b2`), a bare `0b`, and a
                    trailing underscore (`1_`). Tracked as a lexer bug — see
                    docs/SPEC_RECONCILIATION.md round 2, items 10-11. *)
FLOAT_LIT    = digit { digit } "." digit { digit } [ ("e"|"E") ["+"|"-"] digit { digit } ]
                 (* the lexer accepts `1.2.3`, parsed as member-access
                    chaining on a float literal. Lexer bug — same tracking. *)
STRING_LIT   = '"' { char_escape | printable_char - '"' } '"'
BOOL_LIT     = "true" | "false"

(* There are NO character literals. CHAR_LIT was removed after conformance
   round 1 confirmed the lexer/parser have no char-literal support. *)
```

Note on identifier case: the productions below distinguish `IDENT` from
`UPPER_IDENT` as the **normative convention** (types, variants, effects, and
actors are PascalCase; values and functions are camelCase). The reference
parser is case-insensitive in most of these positions (conformance round 2,
items 12 and 23); that leniency is accepted and documented in
`docs/SPEC_RECONCILIATION.md` — it is not a license to write lowercase type
names in new code.

Reserved keywords (not usable as identifiers): `fn`, `let`, `if`, `then`,
`else`, `match`, `with`, `in`, `return`, `type`, `alias`, `enum`, `struct`,
`effect`, `handle`, `perform`, `actor`, `behavior`, `spawn`, `send`,
`receive`, `val`, `iso`, `trn`, `box`, `tag`, `lineariso`, `true`, `false`,
`nil`, `unit`, `pub`, `extern`, `where`, `priv`, `loop`, `node`, `monitor`,
`link`, `exit`, `await`, `subworkflow`, `case`, `do`, `var`, `not`.
(`resume` and `after` are contextual: `resume` only inside a handle arm,
`after` only after a `receive` block.)

## 2. Core productions (Frozen — RFC 0002)

A Core program is a sequence of declarations. Core excludes actors, all
effects except `IO.print`/`IO.read`, and all capabilities except `val`.

```
program        = { decl SEP } ;
fn_decl        = [ "pub" ] "fn" IDENT [ type_params ] "(" [ param_list ] ")"
                 [ "->" type ] [ fn_tail ] [ ":" cap ] block ;
                 (* return-type sigil is "->" (TokenKind::Arrow);
                    src/parser.rs parse_function, consume_if(Arrow) at :654.
                    fn_tail: after the return type, `! {Row}` is an EFFECT
                    ROW, while `! E` / `throws E` (non-braced) is an ERROR
                    TYPE, optionally followed by a second `!`/`throws` effect
                    row (src/parser.rs:892-921). The same `! E` surface in a
                    nested arrow TYPE is an effect row instead — see
                    arrow_type below and round-2 drift item 5. *)
fn_tail        = ( "!" | "throws" )
                 ( effect_row | type [ ( "!" | "throws" ) effect_row ] ) ;
decl           = fn_decl
                | type_decl          (* enum / struct / variant *)
                | alias_decl
                | const_decl ;

param_list     = param { "," param } ;
param          = [ cap ] IDENT [ ":" type ] ;
                 (* capability annotations on parameters are PREFIX:
                    `fn f(val x: Int)` (src/parser.rs try_parse_param_capability
                    :5871, parse_params :5895). The postfix form
                    `x: Int @ val` is REJECTED — round-2 drift item 1. *)

type_decl      = [ "pub" ] "type" UPPER_IDENT [ type_params ]
                  ( variant_body | record_body ) ;
type_params    = "[" IDENT { "," IDENT } "]" ;
variant_body   = [ "|" ] variant { "|" variant } ;
variant        = UPPER_IDENT [ "(" type ")" ] ;
                 (* a variant constructor carries at most ONE payload type;
                    multi-payload `A(Int, Int)` is rejected
                    (src/parser.rs parse_variants :5752) — round-1 drift. *)
record_body    = "{" { field SEP } "}" ;
field          = IDENT ":" type ;
alias_decl     = [ "pub" ] "type" "alias" UPPER_IDENT [ type_params ] "=" type ;
                 (* the `type` keyword is mandatory; bare `alias Name = T` is
                    rejected — round-1 drift. *)
const_decl     = [ "pub" ] "const" IDENT [ ":" type ] "=" expr ;

(* Expressions — Pratt precedence; listed lowest to highest binding.
   Operator table verified against src/parser.rs:31-46 (PREC_*) and the
   infix table at :81-94. NOTE (quirk, adopted): shifts and the bitwise
   operators `&`, `^`, `|||` bind TIGHTER than arithmetic, so
   `1 + 2 & 3` parses as `1 + (2 & 3)`. Already documented in SPEC2 §2.6
   and Appendix E item 3; resolution is deferred to an RFC. *)
expr           = assign_expr ;
assign_expr    = range_expr [ "=" assign_expr ] ;        (* only in `let`/`const` *)
                 (* there is NO compound assignment: `+=` / `-=` are lexed
                    but rejected at parse time ("Not a binary operator:
                    PlusAssign/MinusAssign", src/parser.rs:3019) — round-1
                    drift. Lvalue checking is semantic, not syntactic:
                    `1 = 2` parses (accepted leniency, round-2 item 16). *)
range_expr     = or_expr { ".." or_expr } ;              (* half-open range *)
or_expr        = and_expr { "||" and_expr } ;
and_expr       = eq_expr { "&&" eq_expr } ;
eq_expr        = cmp_expr { ( "==" | "!=" ) cmp_expr } ;
cmp_expr       = add_expr { ( "<" | ">" | "<=" | ">=" ) add_expr } ;
add_expr       = mul_expr { ( "+" | "-" ) mul_expr } ;
mul_expr       = shift_expr { ( "*" | "/" | "%" ) shift_expr } ;
shift_expr     = bitand_expr { ( "<<" | ">>" ) bitand_expr } ;
bitand_expr    = bitxor_expr { "&" bitxor_expr } ;
bitxor_expr    = bitor_expr { "^" bitor_expr } ;
bitor_expr     = exp_expr { "|||" exp_expr } ;
                 (* bitwise-or is `|||`; single `|` is reserved for
                    arm/pattern syntax — round-1 drift. *)
exp_expr       = unary_expr [ "**" exp_expr ] ;          (* right-assoc power *)
unary_expr     = ( "-" | "!" | "not" | "&" | "*" ) unary_expr | primary ;

primary        = INT_LIT | FLOAT_LIT | STRING_LIT | BOOL_LIT
                | "nil" | "unit"
                | IDENT
                | "(" expr { "," expr } ")"            (* paren or tuple *)
                | block
                | if_expr
                | match_expr
                | lambda
                | record_literal
                | call_expr ;

call_expr      = primary "(" [ expr { "," expr } ")" ] ;  (* desugared in Pratt *)
record_literal = "{" { field_init SEP } "}" ;
field_init     = IDENT [ ":" expr ] ;                     (* `x` desugars to `x: x` *)

if_expr        = "if" expr ( "then" ( block | expr ) | expr )
                 [ "else" ( if_expr | block ) ] ;
                 (* `then` is REQUIRED when the then-branch is a `{ }` block
                    and optional before a bare expression
                    (src/parser.rs parse_if :3563-3578) — round-1 drift. *)
match_expr     = "match" expr [ "with" ] "{" { match_arm SEP } "}" ;
match_arm      = [ "case" ] [ "|" ] pattern [ "if" expr ] "=>" expr ;
                 (* both `case` and `|` arm prefixes are accepted, as are
                    `if` guards (src/parser.rs parse_match :3615-3633). *)
pattern        = "_" | INT_LIT | STRING_LIT | BOOL_LIT
                | IDENT | UPPER_IDENT [ "(" pattern ")" ]
                (* single-payload constructor patterns, matching variant *)
lambda         = "fn" "(" [ param_list ] ")" [ "->" [ type ] ] block ;
                 (* in lambda position `->` may appear WITHOUT a type
                    (round-2 item 21); `->` still requires a type in
                    fn_decl. *)
block          = "{" { stmt SEP } [ expr ] "}" ;
stmt           = let_stmt | return_stmt | expr ;
let_stmt       = "let" IDENT [ ":" type ] "=" expr ;
                 (* no capability annotations on `let`: both
                    `let val x = 1` and `let x: Int @ val = 1` are rejected
                    — round-2 drift item 2. *)
return_stmt    = "return" [ expr ] ;

(* Types *)
type           = arrow_type ;
arrow_type     = atomic_type [ "->" arrow_type [ "!" effect_row ] [ ":" cap ] ] ;
                 (* right-associative; the effect row / capability attach to
                    the arrow as a whole (src/parser.rs parse_type_arrow
                    :4933-4953). In TYPE position, `!` always introduces an
                    effect row (never an error type). *)
atomic_type    = UPPER_IDENT [ type_args ]    (* named: Int, Bool, String, Vec<T>, ... *)
                | "(" [ type { "," type } ] ")" ;
type_args      = "[" type { "," type } "]" ;

effect_row     = "{" [ IDENT { "," IDENT } [ "," "|" IDENT ] | "|" IDENT ] "}" ;
                 (* OPEN rows require a comma before the row bar when
                    concrete effects precede it: `{IO, | r}`. `{IO | r}` is
                    rejected; `{| r}` is fine (src/parser.rs parse_effect_row
                    :5588) — round-2 drift item 4. In type position a bare
                    effect name (`Int -> Int ! IO`) is also accepted as a
                    closed row. *)

SEP            = newline | ";" ;
```

## 3. Stable extensions (Stable tier — change requires RFC + deprecation)

These productions extend the Core grammar. They are Stable: breaking changes
require an RFC and a deprecation cycle.

```
(* Effects (beyond IO) *)
decl          += effect_decl ;
effect_op     = IDENT ":" [ "(" [ type { "," type } ] ")" ] "->" type ;
perform_expr  = "perform" UPPER_IDENT "." IDENT "(" [ expr_list ] ")" ;
primary       += perform_expr ;
primary       += handle_expr ;
handle_expr   = "handle" expr [ "with" [ IDENT ] ] "{" { handle_arm SEP } "}" ;
                (* `with` is optional; `with name` references a prior
                   `handler name { ... }` declaration (src/parser.rs
                   parse_handle, consume_if(With) at :4182) *)
handle_arm    = [ "|" ] UPPER_IDENT "." IDENT "(" [ IDENT { "," IDENT } ] ")"
                [ "resume" ] "=>" expr ;
                (* arm parameters are BARE names (no type annotations), and
                   handle arms have NO `if` guards — unlike match, receive,
                   and catch arms (src/parser.rs parse_handle :4453-4484;
                   round-2 drift item 7). `case` is not an arm prefix here. *)
cap           = "val" | "iso" | "trn" | "ref" | "box" | "tag"
              | "lineariso" | "linear" ;
                (* full set per src/parser.rs parse_capability :5546 *)
(* Capability-annotated parameters are the PREFIX form in §2 (`param`).
   The postfix forms `x: Int @ val` and `let x: Int @ val = ...` from an
   earlier draft of this section were removed in reconciliation round 2:
   the parser rejects both (round-2 drift items 1-2). *)

(* Error handling (Stable; slated for removal under DRAFT RFC 0015 —
   the productions below describe the parser as it exists today) *)
catch_expr    = "catch" expr ( expr | "{" { catch_arm SEP } "}" ) ;
catch_arm     = [ "|" ] pattern [ "if" expr ] "=>" expr ;
                (* catch arms accept `|` prefixes and `if` guards but NOT
                   the `case` keyword (round-2 drift item 6). An empty arm
                   block `catch e { }` parses (accepted leniency, item 20).
                   The postfix form `expr catch expr` desugars identically. *)
primary       += catch_expr ;

(* Actors — the universal abstraction (SPEC2 Ch. 8) *)
decl          += actor_decl ;
actor_decl    = [ "pub" ] [ "persistent" ] "actor" IDENT "{" { actor_member SEP } "}" ;
actor_member  = state_field | behavior ;
                (* the parser also accepts the event-sourcing members
                   `initial`, `version: N`, `events { ... }`, `apply`, and
                   `migration` (src/parser.rs parse_actor :1003-1086; see
                   SPEC2 Ch. 9). `fn` declarations are REJECTED inside actor
                   bodies — round-2 drift item 8. *)
state_field   = "state" [ state_model ] IDENT [ ":" type ] "=" expr ;
                (* the state model is a PREFIX keyword —
                   `state durable b: Int = 0`; there is no `@ model` form
                   (src/parser.rs parse_state_model :2031, parse_actor
                   :1009-1023; conformance/grammar/positive_actors_round2.nula
                   case 18). The `=` initializer is required. *)
state_model   = "local" | "durable" | "event_sourced" | "crdt" [ IDENT ] ;
                (* `crdt` may be followed by a CRDT type selector
                   (`gcounter`, `pncounter`, `gset`, ...) — parse_state_model *)
behavior      = "behavior" IDENT "(" [ param_list ] ")" [ "->" type ]
                [ fn_tail ] [ ":" cap ] block ;

(* Actor expressions *)
spawn_expr    = "spawn" [ "link" | "monitor" ] [ "@" expr ] IDENT
                [ "(" [ expr { "," expr } ] ")" ] [ "{" [ init_fields ] "}" ] ;
                (* `link`/`monitor` are PREFIX modifiers desugared at parse
                   time (src/parser.rs parse_spawn :3839-3900). A capability
                   between `spawn` and the actor name (`spawn iso Counter {}`)
                   is REJECTED — round-2 drift item 3; capability-of-spawned-
                   reference syntax is reserved for a future RFC. *)
send_expr     = "send" [ "remote" ] expr IDENT "(" [ expr { "," expr } ] ")" ;
                (* keyword form, src/parser.rs parse_send_keyword :3721.
                   Postfix form `actor ! behavior(args)` (equivalently
                   `actor <- behavior(args)`) is a Pratt postfix operator
                   in parse_expr_with_prec (:2466, :2483); both desugar to
                   the same Expr::Send node. There is no `send a to b`
                   production. *)
receive_expr  = "receive" "{" { receive_arm SEP } "}"
                [ "after" expr "=>" expr ] ;
                (* zero arms (`receive {}`) parse, and the after-body is any
                   expression, not only a block (round-2 item 13). `after` is
                   a contextual identifier, not a keyword. *)
primary       += spawn_expr | send_expr | receive_expr ;
receive_arm   = [ "|" ] IDENT "(" [ pattern { "," pattern } ] ")"
                [ "if" expr ] "=>" expr ;
                (* arm parameters are PATTERNS and arms accept `if` guards
                   (src/parser.rs parse_receive :4558). *)
(* Pipe operator *)
pipe_expr     = primary "|>" IDENT [ "." IDENT ] ;
primary       += pipe_expr ;     (* left-associative, desugars to call *)
```

## 4. Out of scope (Experimental tier)

The following surface is Experimental and deliberately **not** specified here.
It may change without RFC and is not part of the frozen or stable language:

- `workflow` and `agent` declarations (AI orchestration).
- `@backend(native|wasm)` actor annotation.
- `@tool(description: "...")` function annotation (LLM tool exposure).
- `extern "lib" { ... }` FFI blocks.
- `perform Python.call(...)` and the `python` feature surface.
- `@persistent` and the durable-execution syntax beyond the `@durable`
  state model (which is Stable).
- WASM/WIT integration, the `nula` package manager CLI grammar.

When an Experimental feature stabilizes, its productions are added to §3
under an RFC and removed from this list.

## 5. Parser conformance

The reference parser (`src/parser.rs`) must accept every string in the language
of §2 ∪ §3 and reject every string outside it (modulo Experimental §4). The
`conformance/grammar/` corpus contains positive cases (must parse) and negative
cases (must reject); `conformance/run.rs` executes them against the current
parser. A drift between this grammar and the parser is a bug in whichever was
not most recently updated under an RFC. Drift discovered by conformance rounds
is dispositioned in `docs/SPEC_RECONCILIATION.md`; known parser leniencies that
are accepted but NOT part of this grammar (trailing commas, case-insensitive
identifier positions, expression-position `let` chaining without separators,
`resume(...)` outside handler arms, degenerate empty blocks) are listed there
rather than encoded above, so this grammar remains the normative target.
